      "text": "The unit **$\\mu\\text{U/ml}$** stands for **micro-International Units per milliliter**. It is the standard clinical measurement used to describe the concentration of insulin in your blood.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what that actually means in plain English:\n\n### 1. The Symbols\n*   **$\\mu$ (micro):** One-millionth ($1/1,000,000$).\n*   **U (International Unit):** A measure of biological \"power\" or effect.\n*   **ml (milliliter):** A small volume of liquid (about 1/5th of a teaspoon).\n\n### 2. Why \"Units\" instead of \"Milligrams\"?\nMost medications are measured by weight (like a 500mg Aspirin). However, biological substances like insulin are measured by **activity**. \n*   An **International Unit (IU)** is defined by how much a specific amount of the hormone actually *does* in the body (specifically, how much it lowers blood sugar). \n*   Historically, 1 Unit was defined as the amount of insulin needed to lower the blood sugar of a 2kg rabbit by a specific amount. Today, it is standardized so that **1 Unit $\\approx$ 0.0347 mg** of pure human insulin.\n\n### 3. What is a \"Normal\" amount?\nTo give you a sense of scale for the numbers in the paper:\n*   **Fasting (Basal) Level:** A healthy person usually has a concentration of **2 to 10 $\\mu\\text{U/ml}$** while fasting. \n*   **After a Meal:** This can spike to **50–100 $\\mu\\text{U/ml}$** or higher as the pancreas releases insulin to handle the food.\n\n### 4. Why it matters for this paper\nIn the model equations you asked about ($k_{GL}$, $k_{AA}$, etc.), the authors use $\\mu\\text{U/ml}$ because they are looking at the **concentration** of insulin in the plasma. \n*   When they say $k_{GL} \u003d 0.0974$, they are saying: \"For every 1 mg/dl increase in glucose, the insulin concentration in the blood will rise by 0.0974 micro-units every minute.\"\n\n### Summary Table\n| Unit | Full Name | Type of Measure |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **$\\mu\\text{U/ml}$** | micro-International Units per milliliter | **Concentration** (How \"crowded\" the insulin is in your blood) |\n| **mU/L** | milli-International Units per liter | **The same value** as $\\mu\\text{U/ml}$ (mathematically identical) |\n| **pmol/L** | picomoles per liter | The **Scientific (SI) unit**. ($1 \\mu\\text{U/ml} \\approx 6.0 \\text{ pmol/L}$) |",
